WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
Then evaluate it with models in the loop.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:07.512
A server that passes every deterministic test can still be hard for a model to use.

3
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:15.643
The model might call search_issues with a whole sentence, skip get_issue and guess from summaries, or call the write tool without the preview.

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:27.357
These are description and design problems, and Anthropic’s guide to writing tools for agents recommends finding them the way you find any prompt problem: with realistic tasks and transcripts.

5
00:00:27.637 --> 00:00:31.883
Write ten or so tasks that people would really ask: “What’s blocking launch?

6
00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:35.064
”, “Is anything assigned to Dana overdue for an update?

7
00:00:35.344 --> 00:00:38.011
”, “Move HAR-106 to in progress.

8
00:00:38.291 --> 00:00:43.752
” Run each in the host, read what the model called and in what order, and note failures by type.

9
00:00:44.032 --> 00:00:47.552
A tool chosen wrongly usually means overlapping descriptions.

10
00:00:47.832 --> 00:00:51.715
Invalid arguments usually mean a parameter description is vague.

11
00:00:51.995 --> 00:00:55.536
Too many calls usually mean results are too thin or too long.

12
00:00:55.816 --> 00:00:57.309
Fix, rerun, compare.

13
00:00:57.589 --> 00:00:60.000
This is also where model selection enters.

14
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:06.765
Claude Code lets you switch models, and the same server can be connected to other hosts backed by other providers.

15
00:01:07.045 --> 00:01:14.939
Run the same task set across the models you might deploy and compare tool-selection accuracy, unnecessary calls, and cost.

16
00:01:15.219 --> 00:01:23.347
A smaller model that handles your read tools reliably may be the right default, with a larger one for tasks that involve the write tool.

17
00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:26.208
Decide with the transcripts, not the leaderboard.
